problems.problem:
The equation
$$8^{x}+4=4^{x}+2^{x+2}$$
has

(a) no real solutions;
(b) one real solution;
(c) two real solutions;
(d) three real solutions.

The function
$$y=2x^{3}-6x^{2}+5x-7$$
has

(a) no stationary points;
(b) one stationary point;
(c) two stationary points;
(d) three stationary points.

The simultaneous equations in $x,y$,
$$\begin{aligned}(\cos\theta)\,x-(\sin\theta)\,y&=2\\(\sin\theta)\,x+(\cos\theta)\,y&=1\end{aligned}$$
are solvable

(a) for all values of $\theta$ in the range $0\leqslant\theta<2\pi$;
(b) except for one value of $\theta$ in the range $0\leqslant\theta<2\pi$;
(c) except for two values of $\theta$ in the range $0\leqslant\theta<2\pi$;
(d) except for three values of $\theta$ in the range $0\leqslant\theta<2\pi$.

If the trapezium rule is used to estimate the integral
$$\int_{0}^{1}f(x)\,\mathrm{d}x,$$
by splitting the interval $0\leqslant x\leqslant 1$ into $10$ intervals then an overestimate of the integral is produced. It follows that

(a) the trapezium rule with $10$ intervals underestimates $\int_{0}^{1}2f(x)\,\mathrm{d}x$;
(b) the trapezium rule with $10$ intervals underestimates $\int_{0}^{1}(f(x)-1)\,\mathrm{d}x$;
(c) the trapezium rule with $10$ intervals underestimates $\int_{1}^{2}f(x-1)\,\mathrm{d}x$;
(d) the trapezium rule with $10$ intervals underestimates $\int_{0}^{1}(1-f(x))\,\mathrm{d}x$.

In the range $0\leqslant x<2\pi$, the equation
$$2^{\sin^{2}x}+2^{\cos^{2}x}=2$$

(a) has $0$ solutions;
(b) has $1$ solution;
(c) has $2$ solutions;
(d) holds for all values of $x$.

The equation in $x$
$$3x^{4}-16x^{3}+18x^{2}+k=0$$
has four real solutions

(a) when $-27<k<5$;
(b) when $5<k<27$;
(c) when $-27<k<-5$;
(d) when $-5<k<0$.

The polynomial
$$n^{2}x^{2n+3}-25nx^{n+1}+150x^{7}$$
has $x^{2}-1$ as a factor

(a) for no values of $n$;
(b) for $n=10$ only;
(c) for $n=15$ only;
(d) for $n=10$ and $n=15$ only.

The graph $y=f(x)$ of a function is drawn below for $0\leqslant x\leqslant 1$.

The trapezium rule is then used to estimate
$$\int_{0}^{1}f(x)\,\mathrm{d}x$$
by dividing $0\leqslant x\leqslant 1$ into $n$ equal intervals. The estimate calculated will equal the actual integral when

(a) $n$ is a multiple of $4$;
(b) $n$ is a multiple of $6$;
(c) $n$ is a multiple of $8$;
(d) $n$ is a multiple of $12$.

