Let
$$T=\left(\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos x\,\mathrm{d}x\right)\times\left(\int_{\pi}^{2\pi}\sin x\,\mathrm{d}x\right)\times\left(\int_{0}^{\pi/8}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\cos 3x}\right).$$
Which of the following is true?

(a) $T=0$;
(b) $T<0$;
(c) $T>0$;
(d) $T$ is not defined.

There are positive real numbers $x$ and $y$ which solve the equations
$$2x+ky=4,\qquad x+y=k$$
for

(a) all values of $k$;
(b) no values of $k$;
(c) $k=2$ only;
(d) only $k>-2$.

In the region $0<x\leqslant 2\pi$, the equation
$$\int_{0}^{x}\sin(\sin t)\,\mathrm{d}t=0$$
has

(a) no solution;
(b) one solution;
(c) two solutions;
(d) three solutions.

The vertices of an equilateral triangle are labelled $X$, $Y$ and $Z$. The points $X$, $Y$ and $Z$ lie on a circle of circumference $10$ units. Let $P$ and $A$ be the numerical values of the triangle's perimeter and area, respectively. Which of the following is true?

(a) $\dfrac{A}{P}=\dfrac{5}{4\pi}$;
(b) $P<A$;
(c) $\dfrac{P}{A}=\dfrac{10}{3\pi}$;
(d) $P^{2}$ is rational.

If two chords $QP$ and $RP$ on a circle of radius $1$ meet in an angle $\theta$ at $P$, for example as drawn in the diagram below, then the largest possible area of the shaded region $RPQ$ is

(a) $\theta\left(1+\cos\left(\dfrac{\theta}{2}\right)\right)$;
(b) $\theta+\sin\theta$;
(c) $\dfrac{\pi}{2}(1-\cos\theta)$;
(d) $\theta$.

